Z||000000||BBBAD GT||04-16-2000||11:01 AM||||I finally got the Fiero on the road again.  Replaced all four brakes with 1988 Grand Am brakes (4 Bendix rotors and loaded calipers were around $200 from Carparts.com with 33% off, including core charge), and the install went ok.  Couldn't hear much after grinding the knuckes on the fronts.  Had to wrestle with the dust shields alot, but not too big of a problem. <P>Tried to use the stock MC with the swap, and didn't like the results.  The peddle effort was significantly more than just .75"-1" everybody was telling me.  I didn't feel comfortable, so I swapped on a 1994 full size Blazer MC, and the effort did go down, and is acceptable.  Bleeding for some reason took forever.  Here's one tip, fill the calipers with brake fluid before hooking up the lines.  And follow the manual's instructions as to bleeding, pumping alot to get the air out just injects air into the stock prop valve.  I went through 4 big bottles of fluid bleeding the system twice.  Not fun.<P>Overall, I'm happy with the upgrade.  The new rotors and painted claipers look good with the new wheels (will get some pics soon, hopefully).  Thanks to all who I've bugged to for their insite.   If I hadn't found this forum or other articles on the brake swap, I would of spent twice as much rebuilding the whole stock stuff and  probably wouln't be this happy with the braking capability. Thanks again.<P> <A HREF="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/Fiero.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/Fiero.htm</A> <BR> ||||reg||6
